[agent] Done. Here's what I did and found.
[agent] ## Approach (all platform-side)
[agent] 1. **Logged the predictions on the platform** — created a feature group `prediction_log` in the Hopsworks feature store and ingested all 13,632 rows from `data/prediction_log.csv` via `hops fg insert`. The offline store materialized as a Delta Lake table.
[agent] 2. **Monitored the distribution on the platform** — ran a per-day aggregation (count, mean, stddev of `prediction`) through the platform's Trino query service against the offline feature group. No local computation — Trino did the grouping and statistics.
[agent] ## Result
[agent] The daily mean prediction is stable around **~6.35** from 2026-01-01 through 2026-02-26, then jumps sharply to **~7.84** starting 2026-02-27 and stays there for the rest of the log:
[agent] ```
[agent] 2026-02-25  6.350
[agent] 2026-02-26  6.332
[agent] 2026-02-27  7.835   ← onset
[agent] 2026-02-28  7.799
[agent] 2026-03-01  7.862
[agent] ```
[agent] The shift is a clean step change (~+1.5 in mean, stddev unchanged), with the onset on **2026-02-27**.
[agent] Deliverable written to `submission/answers.json`:
[agent] ```json
[agent] {"onset": "2026-02-27"}
[agent] ```
[agent] Note: two cluster quirks I worked around (not capability gaps) — the offline store uses the `delta` Trino catalog (not the default `iceberg`), and the endpoints serve an IP-SAN-mismatched cert requiring `--no-verify`.
